        "## Abstract\n",
        "\n",
        "## Motivation\n",
        "\n",
        "The culinary arts are a complex subject area and an interesting area of potential optimization. It is a difficult domain to model with data due to its many hard-to-measure features, such as the wide range of ingredients, the specificity of heat and time measurements, the various techniques and steps, and the evaluation of the results. Culinary dishes often do not present well as an optimization problem, as maximizing taste or texture, for example, is not objective. As such, there is little applied data science in the domain outside of the privte commercial food industry.\n",
        "\n",
        "One sub-domain that circumvents some of these issues is baking. Many baking recipes involve a small list of ingredients, as few as four in many cases - salt, water, flour, and yeast. Additionally, it is quite common to represent these (and any other ingredients) as \"baker's percentages\", as a percentage of the amount of flour used. These formulations solve the aforementioned specificity and measurement problems. Unfortunately, if we are interested in optimizing these formulations towards a specific outcome, we must be able to describe and label such outcomes (as classes) consistently. For baking broadly, this is not feasible due to the very large number of potential classes. For this reason, we are specifically interested in pizza dough formulations.\n",
        "\n",
        "Pizza needs no introduction; it is one of the most widely recognized and popular foods in the world, with many distinct regional variations, a small and economical ingredient list, and a relatively straightforward product for both consumer and commercial sectors. \n",
        "\n",
        "In particular, consumer interest in at-home pizza making has grown rapidly. Manufacturers specializing in dedicated pizza equipment, such as Ooni and Gozney, have seen remarkable market growth. Gozney Group Limited's filed UK accounts show turnover rising from £12.9M in the financial year ended 31 March 2021 to £60.7M in the year ended 31 March 2024 - four consecutive years of compounding growth, at +111%, +62%, and +37% respectively.[^gozney] Its closest competitor, Ooni, is reported to have reached roughly $200M in annual revenue at its peak, easing to approximately $194M in 2023 as pandemic-era demand normalized.[^ooni]\n",
        "\n",
        "Interest in recipes for specific styles has also increased. Among the rising US pizza dough queries of the five years to September 2026, four of the fifteen highest-volume are Neapolitan-specific: \"neapolitan pizza dough recipe\" and \"neapolitan pizza\" both carry a search-interest index of 24, and \"neapolitan pizza dough\" and \"neapolitan pizza recipe\" both carry 23.[^trends] This indicates real consumer interest in the formulation of specific pizza dough styles.\n",
        "\n",
        "![The fifteen highest-volume rising US pizza dough search queries, September 2021 - September 2026](../../figures/pizza_dough_rising_queries_light.svg){fig-align=\"center\" width=\"90%\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "As such, this paper explores culinary optimization, using pizza a sub-domain, as a machine learning problem, focused on guiding and optimizing pizza dough formulae development. Our goal is to explore the feasibility of using statistical methods and machine learning to classify three popular pizza styles (Neapolitan, New York, and Sicillian) from dough formula, and allow for more efficient development than trial and error.\n",
        "\n",
        "<### Managing Consumer Home Making Variables>\n",
        "\n",
        "This framework in particular supports the consumer who cooks and develops recipes at home, an environment with a much larger range of variables, including;\n",
        "\n",
        "- Oven temperatures & thermostat accuracy\n",
        "- Imprecise volume measurements\n",
        "- Inconsistenty & availability of ingredients\n",
        "- Limited resources for large volumes of samples\n",
        "- Environmental differences, including altitude and humidity\n",
        "- Limited resources \n",
        "\n",
        "These types of variables are difficult control, especially from user to user. As such, we propose that dough formulation is the best tool available to a home cook to achieve a particular outcome. As the data for these factors are not available, we can attempt to normalize them and provide the means for a user to fine-tune a formula to their specific environmental factors.\n",
        "\n",
        "<### Additional Applications to Commercial Users>\n",
        "\n",
        "This is not to say the implications are limited to home users. Commercial users may find it useful for product development, no-cost experimentation, and scaling to their particular operations. For example, a pizzeria that specializes in Neapolitan pizzas made with a particular local flour may wish to explore a New York style offering. They would likely wish to differentiate their products sufficiently, while still using the same local flour. Having the ability to take their base recipe and adjust towards a new distinct offering, while keeping their preferred ingredient list, can reduce experimentation cyles and matertial costs.\n",
        "\n",
        "In short, we expect that our methods could be useful to a wide range of formula developers across sectors.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Background of dough formulation\n",
        "\n",
        "We have focused much on the dough formulation thus far, ignoring other ingredients, proofing times, shaping techniques, and firing temperatures. While these are certaintly interesting features, it is beyond our scope to attempt to encode each of these factors in a standardized way.\n",
        "\n",
        "As mentioned before, baking perccentages are numerical features of a dough formla, allowing us a consistent vector representation expressed as;\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "r = \\big(\\mathbf{p},\\ t_{\\text{flour}},\\ t_{\\text{yeast}},\\ t_{\\text{fat}},\\ t_{\\text{sugar}}\\big)\n",
        "$$ {#eq-recipe}\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $\\mathbf{p}$ holds the baker's percentages. For each ingredient $i \\in \\mathcal{I} = \\{\\text{flour}, \\text{water}, \\text{salt}, \\text{yeast}, \\text{fat}, \\text{sugar}\\}$ with mass $m_i$ in grams, the baker's percentage is its mass relative to the flour:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_i = 100 \\cdot \\frac{m_i}{m_{\\text{flour}}}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-bakers}\n",
        "\n",
        "Flour is therefore always $p_{\\text{flour}} = 100$. A constant carries no information, so it is left out, and the remaining five ratios form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbf{p} = (p_{\\text{water}},\\ p_{\\text{salt}},\\ p_{\\text{yeast}},\\ p_{\\text{fat}},\\ p_{\\text{sugar}}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^5_{\\ge 0}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Each ingredient $i \\in \\mathcal{K} = \\{\\text{flour}, \\text{yeast}, \\text{fat}, \\text{sugar}\\}$ also has a type $t_i$, drawn from a fixed vocabulary $\\mathcal{T}_i$. For example, $\\mathcal{T}_{\\text{flour}} = \\{\\text{all-purpose}, \\text{bread}, \\text{double zero}, \\text{high-gluten}, \\text{semolina}\\}$. The notation used throughout is collected in @tbl-notation.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a natural representation for dough formulas; baking percentages are captured as numerica features, while the classes of these ingredients, such as high-fluten or all-purpose flours, is encoded as categorical. These are the core features that are present in every formula."
