        "We have allowed for common dough enrichments, including fat such as olive oil, and sugars such as refined white sugar. The presence or absence of these optional ingedients can contain valuable information as to the culinary style of the formula.\n",
        "\n",
        "An absent enrichment has both a zero ratio and the type $\\text{none}$, and the two always go together:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_i = 0 \\iff t_i = \\text{none}, \\qquad i \\in \\{\\text{fat}, \\text{sugar}\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### Pizza Styles\n",
        "\n",
        "We have briefly mentioned a few pizza styles thus far. Due to the limitation of data, and popularity of the following styles, we have limited our study to Neapolitan, New York, and Sicillian.\n",
        "\n",
        "Neapolitan is the most clearly defined style of pizza. Originating from Naples, Italy, it is characterized by a soft, pillowy, unenriched dough. Usually smaller than other styles, the base of the crust is thin, with a highly aerated perimiter. Neapolitan pizza is defined very clearly by the Associazione Verace Pizza Napoletana (AVPN) Internal Regulations, however in keeping with other styles, this was not used as a definitive centroid (although it is one of the recipes). Neapolitan pizzas are typically baked in extremely hot specific ovens, fueled by hardwoods.\n",
        "\n",
        "New York Style is another well known style originating in New York City, where it is often served as an affordable street food by the slice. These pies are typically much larger and thinner than other styles, with a crisp even crust, baked in commercial ovens.\n",
        "\n",
        "Sicillian is not quite as well known, but we've chosen it as many other popular styles, including Detroit, are very similar. It also provides a point of contrast to the other two styles - Sicillian pizza uses a much thicker dough than either New York or Neapolitan, and is generally a much more approachable style of pizza to the consumer sector as it does not require such extreme baking temperatures or dough handling technique.\n",
        "\n",
        "There are of course many other styles of pizza, but these are well known and typically recognized by their dough more so than other styles.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
