        "\n",
        "<### Data Collection>\n",
        "\n",
        "Formulations for our targeted styles were gathered ethically, following site scraping rules and requirements. Most recipes were gathered by hand as raw text.\n",
        "\n",
        "The dough formulation portion of the recipe was then extracted and structured in a common schema. Conversions and normalization of the data was performed deterministically in code. \n",
        "\n",
        "<### Data Processing>\n",
        "\n",
        "Recipes were processed by converting nominal measurements of flour, water, salt, and yeast into baker's percentages.\n",
        "\n",
        "Each ingredient mass was divided by the flour mass as in @eq-bakers. When a formula blends several flours, $m_{\\text{flour}}$ is their combined mass.\n",
        "\n",
        "Flour, water, salt, and yeast types were assigned a default value if not specified. Types were normalized to a finite list of options based on our domain knowledge. For example, King Arthur Sir Lancelot is a relatively high-gluten flour product, and as such would be assigned a \"high gluten\" flour type.\n",
        "\n",
        "Sourdough starters, poolishes, or other developed bacterial colonies were converted into respective water, flour, and yeast quantities following expert heuristics.\n",
        "\n",
        "Volume measurements are a common source of inconsistency - these were first converted to metric measurements by expert guided conversions before then computing the remaining vector quantities. Note that formulas using volume measurements are imprecise by nature, and this convention will bias these formulas."
