        "## Feature Space\n",
        "\n",
        "We define here our feature space. \n",
        "\n",
        "Each formula is mapped to a feature vector $\\mathbf{x} = \\phi(r) \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ in two steps: the ratios in $\\mathbf{p}$ are standardized, and the ingredient types are one-hot encoded.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Scaling.** The ingredients sit on very different scales. Water ranges from about 57 to 75%, while yeast ranges from about 0.06 to 1.5%. Yet a 0.5-point change in yeast or salt changes the dough far more than a 0.5-point change in hydration. Unscaled distances would be dominated by water, and as such, each ratio in $\\mathbf{p}$ is therefore standardized against the reference corpus $\\mathcal{D}$, writing $p_i^{(r)}$ for the ratio of ingredient $i$ in recipe $r$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_i = \\frac{p_i - \\mu_i}{\\sigma_i}, \\qquad\n",
        "\\mu_i = \\frac{1}{\\lvert\\mathcal{D}\\rvert} \\sum_{r \\in \\mathcal{D}} p_i^{(r)}, \\qquad\n",
        "\\sigma_i^2 = \\frac{1}{\\lvert\\mathcal{D}\\rvert} \\sum_{r \\in \\mathcal{D}} \\big(p_i^{(r)} - \\mu_i\\big)^2\n",
        "$$ {#eq-zscore}\n",
        "\n",
        "This gives $\\mathbf{z} = (z_{\\text{water}}, z_{\\text{salt}}, z_{\\text{yeast}}, z_{\\text{fat}}, z_{\\text{sugar}})$. After scaling, a difference of one unit means \"one typical spread in that ingredient\", whichever ingredient it is."
      ],
      "id": "dfc72f28"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: tbl-scaling\n",
        "#| tbl-cap: Range and standardization constants of each baker's percentage over the corpus\n",
        "# TABLE: per ingredient in p: min, max, mean (mu_i), standard deviation (sigma_i) over the corpus.\n",
        "# Backs the \"57 to 75%\" / \"0.06 to 1.5%\" figures in the Scaling paragraph and gives the\n",
        "# actual mu_i, sigma_i of @eq-zscore. scaler_stats(pipe) returns mu, sigma.\n",
        "Markdown(\n",
        "    ratio_summary(pipe, recipes)\n",
        "    .rename(index=label, columns={\"min\": \"Min\", \"max\": \"Max\", \"mean\": r\"Mean $\\mu_i$\", \"std\": r\"SD $\\sigma_i$\"})\n",
        "    .rename_axis(\"Ingredient\")\n",
        "    .to_markdown(floatfmt=\".3f\")\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "tbl-scaling",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Ingredient types.** Each type $t_i$ is one-hot encoded as $\\mathbf{h}_i \\in \\{0,1\\}^{\\lvert\\mathcal{T}_i\\rvert}$: a 1 in the position of $t_i$ within $\\mathcal{T}_i$ and 0 elsewhere. With $\\mathcal{T}_{\\text{flour}}$ ordered as listed above, a bread flour formula has $\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{flour}} = (0, 1, 0, 0, 0)$. For fat and sugar, $\\text{none}$ is a level of its own, so an absent enrichment is encoded explicitly rather than as all zeros.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Feature vector.** Stacking the standardized ratios and the type encodings gives the feature vector:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbf{x} = \\phi(r) =\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\mathbf{z} \\\\ \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{flour}} \\\\ \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{yeast}} \\\\ \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{fat}} \\\\ \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{sugar}}\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\in \\mathbb{R}^d,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "d = 5 + \\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{K}} \\lvert\\mathcal{T}_i\\rvert\n",
        "$$ {#eq-features}"
      ],
