        "- The interpretability of supervised models on a small dataset is reduced\n",
        "- We train several classifiers on existing data to see if there's real signal in the formulations themselves\n",
        "- Model outputs\n",
        "- Outlier formulations\n",
        "\n",
        "## Optimization\n",
        "\n",
        "As describe in our motivations, many formula authors have a specific style or outcome in mind when developing a new dough recipe. This typically involves a decent amount of trial-and-error, in which other uncontrollable factors can be unintentionally introduced. Iterating in this way requires time, supplies, and introduces a degree of randomness to the outcome.\n",
        "\n",
        "To address this, we introduce an optimization algorithm that attempts to fine-tune a formulation, given a starting formulation and target style, by minimizing the distance between input formulation and a point in formula space (e.g. a style centroid).\n",
        "\n",
        "This may sound like a simple exercise, however we've considered two points we believe to be important to a user. Firstly, a user has a specific environment which is difficult to comprehensively measure; availability of ingredients, altitude, humidity, equipment, and so on. We assume that the user has some starting formulation they are content with, but that they would like to adjust - this initial input formulation is assumed to encode these environment variables to some degree. Under this assumption, the algorithm attempts to shrink the style distance *minimally*, such that much of the author's original recipe is perserved, while still providing new ingredient vectors that are closer to the target style. \n",
        "\n",
        "Secondly, users may wish to retain some ingredients that are not present in a typical style. As an example, many people will not have access to commercial flours commonly used in New York style pizza. Commercial users may advertise their use of local ingredients, including a flour with a specific protein content. A bakery with an organic live fermentation may not want to substitute with commercially produced instant yeast. Thus, the algorithm is also penalized for substituting or removing ingredients entirely.\n",
        "\n",
        "The algorithm and loss function can be described as;\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\tilde{\\mathbf{c}} = \\sum_{s \\in \\mathcal{S}} \\omega_s \\mathbf{c}_s, \\qquad \\omega_s \\ge 0, \\qquad \\sum_{s \\in \\mathcal{S}} \\omega_s = 1\n",
        "$$ {#eq-target}\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal{E}(r) = \\{ i \\in \\mathcal{E} : p_i > 0 \\}, \\qquad\n",
        "\\mathcal{E}_{\\cap} = \\mathcal{E}(r) \\cap \\mathcal{E}(r')\n",
        "$$ {#eq-enrichments}\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "L(r' ; r) = {} & \\lambda_{\\text{target}} \\lVert \\mathbf{x}' - \\tilde{\\mathbf{c}} \\rVert^2\n",
        "+ \\lambda_{\\text{ratio}} \\sum_{i \\in \\{\\text{water}, \\text{salt}, \\text{yeast}\\} \\cup \\mathcal{E}_{\\cap}} \\big(z'_i - z_i\\big)^2 \\\\\n",
        "& + \\lambda_{\\text{type}} \\, \\big\\lvert \\{ i \\in \\{\\text{flour}, \\text{yeast}\\} \\cup \\mathcal{E}_{\\cap} : t'_i \\neq t_i \\} \\big\\rvert \\\\\n",
        "& + \\lambda_{\\text{remove}} \\, \\big\\lvert \\mathcal{E}(r) \\setminus \\mathcal{E}(r') \\big\\rvert\n",
        "+ \\lambda_{\\text{add}} \\, \\big\\lvert \\mathcal{E}(r') \\setminus \\mathcal{E}(r) \\big\\rvert\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-loss}\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell_i = \\min_{r \\in \\mathcal{D} :\\, p_i^{(r)} > 0} p_i^{(r)}, \\qquad\n",
        "u_i = \\max_{r \\in \\mathcal{D}} p_i^{(r)}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-bounds}\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "r^{*} = \\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{r'} \\ & L(r' ; r) \\\\\n",
        "\\text{subject to} \\ & t'_i \\in \\mathcal{T}_i, && i \\in \\mathcal{K} \\\\\n",
        "& \\ell_i \\le p'_i \\le u_i, && i \\in \\{\\text{water}, \\text{salt}, \\text{yeast}\\} \\cup \\mathcal{E}(r') \\\\\n",
        "& p'_i = 0, && i \\in \\mathcal{E} \\setminus \\mathcal{E}(r')\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-optimization}\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{clip}_i(v) = \\min\\left( \\max\\left( v,\\ \\frac{\\ell_i - \\mu_i}{\\sigma_i} \\right),\\ \\frac{u_i - \\mu_i}{\\sigma_i} \\right)\n",
        "$$ {#eq-clip}\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z'_i =\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "\\operatorname{clip}_i\\left( \\dfrac{\\lambda_{\\text{target}} \\tilde{c}_i + \\lambda_{\\text{ratio}} z_i}{\\lambda_{\\text{target}} + \\lambda_{\\text{ratio}}} \\right), & i \\in \\{\\text{water}, \\text{salt}, \\text{yeast}\\} \\cup \\mathcal{E}_{\\cap} \\\\[2ex]\n",
        "\\operatorname{clip}_i\\big( \\tilde{c}_i \\big), & i \\in \\mathcal{E}(r') \\setminus \\mathcal{E}(r) \\\\[1ex]\n",
        "-\\mu_i / \\sigma_i, & i \\in \\mathcal{E} \\setminus \\mathcal{E}(r')\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-ratio-solution}\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbf{t}^{*} = \\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{\\mathbf{t}' \\in \\mathcal{T}_{\\text{flour}} \\times \\mathcal{T}_{\\text{yeast}} \\times \\mathcal{T}_{\\text{fat}} \\times \\mathcal{T}_{\\text{sugar}}} L\\big(r'(\\mathbf{t}') ; r\\big)\n",
        "$$ {#eq-type-search}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "- Do pizza dough formulations carry enough information to indicate a specific style, in absence of other recipe parameters?\n",
        "- Are expert labels consistent and can a style be narrowly defined?\n",
